.\" The manual page for @NAME@, in mdoc(7): every line starting with a dot
.\" is a macro that says what the text is (a name, a flag, an argument),
.\" not how it looks. Lines like this one are comments. `make manlint`
.\" checks the file; `man ./@NAME@.1` shows it. Pages to compare with:
.\" /usr/share/man/man1/cat.1 and the mdoc(7) manual itself.
.\"
.\" The date: CVS rewrites $Mdocdate$ on each commit; here it is the day
.\" the page was made. Change it when the page changes.
.Dd $Mdocdate: @DATE@ $
.\" Title and section, in capitals: section 1 is for user commands.
.Dt @UPNAME@ 1
.\" The operating system, filled in by mandoc.
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm @NAME@
.\" One line, lower case, no full stop: this is what apropos searches.
.Nd one line saying what it does
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.\" The same as the usage() line in main.c. Options: .Op Fl x
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility
.\" Say what it does. For each option, a list item:
.\" .Bl -tag -width Ds
.\" .It Fl x
.\" What -x does.
.\" .El
does nothing yet.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.\" .Ex -std writes the usual sentence: 0 on success, >0 on error.
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr style 9
